<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Evaluating the Sensitivity of LLMs to Harmful Contents in Long Input
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Faeze Ghorbanpour
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alexander Fraser
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computation, Information and Technology, TU Munich
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Munich Center for Machine Learning (MCML)
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">faeze.ghorbanpour@tum.de</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) increasingly support applications that rely on extended context, from document processing to retrieval-augmented generation. While their long-context capabilities are well studied for reasoning and retrieval, little is known about their behavior in safety-critical scenarios. We evaluate LLMs’ sensitivity to harmful content under extended context, varying type (explicit vs. implicit), position (beginning, middle, end), prevalence (0.01--0.50 of the prompt), and context length (600--6000 tokens). Across harmful content categories such as toxic, offensive, and hate speech, with LLaMA-3, Qwen-2.5, and Mistral, we observe similar patterns: performance peaks at moderate harmful prevalence (0.25) but declines when content is very sparse or dominant; recall decreases with increasing context length; harmful sentences at the beginning are generally detected more reliably; and explicit content is more consistently recognized than implicit. These findings provide the first systematic view of how LLMs prioritize and calibrate harmful content in long contexts, highlighting both their emerging strengths and the challenges that remain for safety-critical use.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        The evaluation code will be made publicly available.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs have grown rapidly and received considerable attention due to their ability to follow human instructions. Recently, their context length has also expanded, from earlier models such as Flan-T5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which was limited to 512 tokens, to more recent models like Gemini, which can process substantially longer input sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemini Team, 2025</a>)</cite>.
As a result, LLMs are increasingly used in scenarios where long sequences of mixed content must be processed at once <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While most recent work has focused on improving and benchmarking reasoning or retrieval under extended context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuratov et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, less attention has been given to their safety and sensitivity to harmful content in the same setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In real-world deployments, harmful sentences are rarely encountered in isolation; they can be embedded within long social media threads, community forums, multi-user conversations, or large documents, among neutral, unrelated, and noisy contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pavlopoulos et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pérez et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Assessing LLMs’ sensitivity to harmful content is important for
content moderation systems that handle long discussion threads, as well as forum and community managers dealing with multi-turn exchanges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinan et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.
Their failure can expose end-users to harmful outputs and weaken moderation efforts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vidgen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
Document processing and retrieval-augmented generation systems, where harmful passages may appear within extended inputs, also need to detect harmful parts of their content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BehnamGhader et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. It is also important for processing massive corpora for pre-training or fine-tuning language models to ensure that harmful material is identified and removed across long inputs before feeding them to the models for training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stranisci and Hardmeier, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mendu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. All of these domains face the challenge of detecting harmful content that is diluted, implicit, or dispersed throughout extended text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Smaller encoder-based models, although effective after fine-tuning, heavily rely on labeled data and struggle to generalize beyond the domain of the training set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. They also cannot process entire documents at once due to their short context length <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaiswal and Milios, 2023</a>)</cite>. Likewise, evaluating long texts one sentence at a time using LLMs is possible, but it requires excessive prompting, inflates costs, and overlooks how harmful content interacts with its surrounding text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Using LLMs that support long-context input can be a solution in these cases, which motivates studying their performance in long-context mode for a more realistic and scalable assessment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While a few recent works have investigated LLM safety in extended contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, these studies primarily assess overall output safety rates and targeted alignment strategies. They do not provide systematic evidence on how models handle harmful content when multiple factors—such as prevalence, position, and implicitness—interact with context length. We address this gap by evaluating widely used instruction-tuned LLMs on toxic content, offensive language, and hate speech, analyzing how their sensitivity to harmful content shifts under varied long-context conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we systematically analyze LLMs such as LLaMA-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Qwen-2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Mistral’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> behavior when harmful content is enclosed in multi-sentence prompts, focusing on the following aspects:
<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Coverage:</span> Can LLMs accurately extract all harmful instances from a multi-sentence prompt?
<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Prevalence:</span> How does the ratio of harmful to non-harmful content (from 0.05 to 0.50 of the prompt) affect performance?
<span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Dilution:</span> Does performance degrade as prompts grow longer and harmful sentences become increasingly sparse?
<span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Region effect:</span> Does the position of harmful content (beginning, middle, end) influence detection?
<span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(5) Type sensitivity:</span> Does performance differ between explicit and implicit harmful content?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments reveal consistent patterns in how LLMs handle harmful content in long prompts.
Coverage is affected by dilution, with recall decreasing as more non-harmful sentences are added.
Prevalence plays a key role: models achieve their best balance of precision and recall when harmful content makes up around a quarter of the prompt, while performance drops at very low or very high ratios.
Region effects also appear, with harmful content detected most reliably at the beginning, although middle positions sometimes perform comparably in longer contexts.
Finally, explicit content is recognized more consistently than implicit, as it often contains clear lexical cues, while implicit harms rely on subtler semantic inference, making them harder across datasets and models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Evaluation on Long-Context of LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in LLMs have extended their ability to process increasingly long inputs, from earlier architectures such as Longformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beltagy et al. (2020)</a></cite>, BigBird <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al. (2020)</a></cite>, and ETC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ainslie et al., 2020</a>)</cite> to more recent instruction-tuned models like Gemini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemini Team (2025)</a></cite>, which support tens of thousands of tokens in a single prompt. This progress has motivated a wave of long-context evaluation studies, spanning general understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuratov et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Costa-jussà et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, the safety dimension of long-context use remains far less explored, despite the widespread deployment of these models and their societal impact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, as well as growing evidence of safety risks in extended inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anil et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Upadhayay et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Harmful Content Detection with LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work has extensively studied the detection of harmful content at the sentence level, often using fine-tuned encoder models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghorbanpour et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. These approaches rely on manually annotated datasets, which are costly to create <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and susceptible to annotation biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Denton et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and limit generalization across cultures, languages, and contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malik et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
An alternative line of work leverages LLMs in inference mode, prompted without additional fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghorbanpour et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Thanks to pretraining on large and diverse corpora, these models capture nuanced language patterns and generalize across domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattacharya et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Latif et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, making them promising for moderation tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang, 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, such evaluations have almost exclusively focused on the sentence level. Their performance on long inputs—closer to important real-world scenarios, such as long social media threads, community forums, and multi-user conversation moderation—remains largely unexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Harmful Content in Extended Contexts</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has highlighted that harmful content detection and safety issues become more complex when language models are exposed to extended contexts. SafetyBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, Decoding Trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, and HELM Safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaiyom et al., 2024</a>)</cite> revealed broad vulnerabilities even in short prompts. LongSafety by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2025)</a></cite> shows that models’ safety rates degrade with longer inputs, though they can be partially mitigated via targeted alignment. LongSafetyBench by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> further explore alignment degradation in long-context settings, emphasizing the need for deeper alignment beyond surface-level tuning. While their work focuses on compliance scores (i.e. the binary measure of whether a model’s output obeys a safety policy), our study goes further by localizing harmful content and measuring the proportion and placement of dispersed harmful spans. We introduce a dilution analysis, varying context length and prevalence of harmful content, to quantify how sparsity and interleaving affect detection. This offers a more fine-grained lens than compliance metrics alone, giving actionable insight into where models fail in extended texts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Evaluation Design</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of our evaluation is to isolate how different variables—prompt length, harm ratio, harm type, and position—affect LLMs’ sensitivity to harmful content. Because no existing long-context dataset provides this level of controlled variation, we construct prompts artificially by randomly sampling harmful and non-harmful sentences. This setup allows us to vary each factor independently across multiple trials while holding other factors constant, which would be impossible with coherent documents. While the resulting prompts lack discourse structure, this simplification serves as a controlled stress test: it removes confounds such as topic flow or domain-specific context and ensures that performance differences can be attributed directly to the variables under study. We view this abstraction as complementary to evaluations on coherent documents, which remain important but require different resources and objectives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define our evaluation in terms of four variables that control how prompts are constructed: prompt length (<math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), harm ratio (<math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ (0\leq r\leq 1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.500em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ (0\leq r\leq 1)</annotation></semantics></math>
), harm region, and harm type.
In some experiments, instead of <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, we vary the number of sentences (<math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>). Likewise, instead of fixing <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we sometimes vary the number of harmful sentences (<math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>).
For each setting, we run <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> trials with different random seeds. In each trial, harmful sentences are sampled according to <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to fill <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\times r</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (or <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sentences), with the remainder filled by non-harmful sentences. These are then distributed within the specified region <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we number the sentences in ascending order and combine them into a constructed prompt, which is appended to an instruction asking the LLM to identify the harmful sentences by their indices. The model output is thus a list of sentence numbers, which we compare against the ground-truth indices of harmful sentences in the prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we run <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|p|\times|r|\times|h|\times|t|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p|\times|r|\times|h|\times|t|</annotation></semantics></math> settings, each repeated <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times, each with a different random seed, across three instruction-tuned LLMs and three categories of harmful content (toxic content, offensive language, and hate speech) to ensure both breadth and robustness. To evaluate the results, since sentences are sampled randomly from the whole dataset across runs, we aggregate predictions and ground truths over all <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> repetitions of each setting (micro over instances). We consider performance metrics both for harmful content extraction and for overall detection across harmful and non-harmful sentences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt">
<div id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:14.2pt;height:97.7pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:97.7pt;transform:translate(-44.4pt,-44.4pt) rotate(-90deg) ;">
<p id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Sentence-level Prompt</p>
</span></div></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_align_top" style="width:398.3pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Return yes if the sentence is toxic, otherwise no. Definition: Toxic sentence is any rude, disrespectful, hostile, or unreasonable text that is likely</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">to make others leave a conversation.</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Rules: If unsure, output no. Neutral statements, factual content, or polite disagreements are
not toxic. Only answer ’yes’ or ’no’. Nothing else.</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Examples: Sentence: You are an idiot. Answer: yes;</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Sentence: I disagree with your comment. Answer: no;</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Sentence: Muslims don’t belong here. Answer: yes;</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Sentence: I hate this weather. Answer: no;</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p1.1.2.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Sentence: …</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span>
</span></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<div id="S3.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:14.2pt;height:94.2pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:94.2pt;transform:translate(-42.7pt,-42.7pt) rotate(-90deg) ;">
<p id="S3.T1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Long-context Prompt</p>
</span></div></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_align_top" style="width:398.3pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">You will see a numbered list of sentences. Identify which sentences are toxic. Definition: Toxic sentence is any rude, disrespectful, hostile, or</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">unreasonable text
that is likely to make others leave a conversation.</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Rules: If unsure, output nothing. Neutral statements, factual content, or polite disagreements are not toxic. Return only a comma-separated list</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">of their numbers in ascending order.</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Example:</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Sentences: 7. I hate this weather. 10. Muslims don’t belong here. 16. You are stupid.
32. I disagree with your comment.</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Answer: 10, 16</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.p2.1.2.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Sentences: …</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span>
</span></span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Prompt instructions for toxicity in two evaluation settings: sentence-level and long-context.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use three datasets: <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">IHC</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">ElSherief et al., 2021</a>)</cite> for hate speech (21,480 sentences, 38.12% labeled as hate speech, of which 86.56% are implicit), <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OffensEval</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caselli et al., 2020</a>)</cite> for offensive language (13,240 sentences, 33.23% offensive, of which 34.05% are implicit), and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">JigsawToxic</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">cjadams et al., 2017</a>)</cite> for toxic content (119,675 sentences, 10.78% toxic, of which 35.01% are implicit).
These datasets are drawn primarily from social media posts (Twitter tweets and Wikipedia comments), where each instance is typically a single sentence with an average length of about 30 tokens. This makes them well-suited for our evaluation of harmful sentence identification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate three widely used instruction-tuned LLMs: <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Meta-llama-3-8B-Instruct" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_upright">https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Meta-llama-3-8B-Instruct</a></span></span></span></span> (8B parameters, 8k context window, developed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite>), <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">3</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_upright">https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</a></span></span></span></span> (7B parameters, 32k context window, trained by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al. (2024)</a></cite>), and <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">4</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_upright">https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3</a></span></span></span></span> (7B parameters, 32k context window, presented by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>).
We use all models in inference mode without any fine-tuning, as our goal is to evaluate their sensitivity rather than adapt them to the task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Variables</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation varies the prompt length <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\{600,1500,3000,6000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>600</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\{600,1500,3000,6000\}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            Results for longer contexts (15k and 30k tokens) are reported in the appendix.</span></span></span> While it is possible to test much longer contexts, the consistent patterns observed across 600–6,000 tokens provide evidence of trends that likely extend further.
We vary harm ratio (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in{0.05,0.1,0.25,0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in{0.05,0.1,0.25,0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>) to simulate different levels of prevalence in real applications, harm region (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in{\text{beginning},\text{middle},\text{end},\text{all}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext>beginning</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext>middle</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>end</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>all</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in{\text{beginning},\text{middle},\text{end},\text{all}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and harm type (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\text{implicit},\text{explicit},\text{both}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext>implicit</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext>explicit</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>both</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\text{implicit},\text{explicit},\text{both}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Each setting is repeated <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=128</annotation></semantics></math> times, with every trial using its own unique random seed, to guarantee that results are stable and robust.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Prompts</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the instruction appended to the constructed, numbered sentences, we provide (i) a definition of the target type of harm, (ii) a clarification distinguishing it from related categories, and (iii) a short example, to ensure that the LLM understands the task and output format. We experimented with multiple prompting strategies and found that this approach led to more consistent adherence to instructions and better performance. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Evaluation Design ‣ Evaluating the Sensitivity of LLMs to Harmful Contents in Long Input" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the prompts we used: the first row is for the sentence-level setting, where each prompt contains a single sentence and the model outputs yes or no; and the second row is for the long-context setting, where the prompt consists of a list of numbered sentences and the model is asked to output the indices of harmful ones. Additional prompts for other types of harm are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompts ‣ Evaluating the Sensitivity of LLMs to Harmful Contents in Long Input" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As for evaluation metrics, we consider several complementary measures. Since harmful instances occur less frequently than non-harmful ones in our datasets and evaluation settings, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Macro-F1</span>
is a key metric, as it accounts for both classes fairly under imbalance.
To inspect model bias toward the harmful class, we track the <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Predicted Prevalence Value (PPV)</span>, i.e., the proportion of instances predicted as harmful. Finally, because our focus is on models’ sensitivity to harmful content, we also report <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Precision and Recall</span> for the harmful class.
We primarily report pooled (micro) metrics, which treat all sampled sentences across runs as one evaluation set. For completeness, we also computed per-run (macro) averages, but found only negligible differences in the results because all runs are equally sized and drawn from the same distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Sentence-level</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the primary results and to establish whether LLMs can recognize harmful content, we first evaluate in a sentence-level setting, where sentences are prompted one by one. Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Sentence-level ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Evaluating the Sensitivity of LLMs to Harmful Contents in Long Input" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the results on the original (imbalanced) datasets. We also provide balanced results, where the number of harmful and non-harmful sentences is equal. For balancing, we downsample non-harmful sentences using five random seeds and report the average performance across runs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Imbalanced</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Balanced</td></tr>
